# Git Configuration and Commit Rules

## Git User Configuration
Before committing, configure git user to identify automated commits:
- Format: `$(whoami) | Cursor.sh | <model>`
- This marks all AI-generated commits as automated

## Commit Message Format
All git commits must follow this strict format:

**Structure:**
1. First line: Brief one-liner summary
2. Blank line
3. List of changes (each prefixed with `- `)
4. Blank line
5. Separator line with exactly 5 dashes: `-----`
6. Technical attribution section

**Technical Attribution Format:**
```
Prompt: {{prompt}}
Context: {{brief_description_of_what_this_code_does}}

Technical details:
- Model: {{specific_llm_model}}
- IDE: Cursor {{cursor_version}}
- Generation method: AI-assisted pair programming
- Code style: {{language_specific_style_guide}}
- Dependencies: {{key_dependencies}}
```

## Push Behavior and Remote Sync
- All git commits SHALL be followed by `git push` if and only if a remote exists
- Do not push if no remote is configured
- After every commit, automatically run `git push` to keep local and remote in sync
- After every merge, automatically run `git push` if local commits are ahead of remote
- Never skip pushing - the remote repository must stay synchronized with local changes
- If push fails due to conflicts or network issues, retry once before reporting the error
- Always push to the tracked remote branch for the current branch
- Use `git push` (without arguments) which will push to the configured upstream branch
